#import "template.typ": *

#let metadata = (
  title: "Crosswind Circuits — Instructional Walkthrough",
  unit: "Instructor Training | Syllabus Walkthrough 10 of 12 — maps to Ab Initio Phase 2, Stage C2B",
  author: "C.Moroney",
  version: "v1.0.0",
  plan_type: "Instructor Training",
)

#let lesson = (
title: [#metadata.title]

,aim: [
Independently prepare and deliver an instructor-standard ground briefing directly from the ab-initio source lesson plan — with no IT demonstration to imitate first — then fly an instructor-standard in-flight lesson on crosswind circuits, to a standard the IC could use with a real ab-initio student, and be ready to conduct this lesson opportunistically whenever crosswind conditions arise.
]

,overview: [
Maps to Ab Initio Phase 2, Stage C2B (*"Crosswind Circuits"* — see `phase2-stage-C2B - Crosswind Circuits` for full ab-initio content, tolerances, and homework). Unlike most walkthrough lessons, C2B is weather-dependent and is often taught concurrently with or shortly after C2A depending on when crosswind conditions arise — the IC needs to be ready to teach this lesson on short notice, not only on a scheduled day.

This lesson introduces a structural change from Lessons 1–9: for the ground brief, the IC works directly from the ab-initio source lesson plan (reviewed ahead of time per Lesson 9 homework) and prepares an independent brief — there is no IT demonstration to recreate. The brief has to come from reading and interpreting the source material alone, the same way it would for a real opportunistic lesson like this one. The in-flight component reverts to the usual pattern: the IT demonstrates both crosswind technique variants (wing-down and crabbed) in full, and the IC recreates the flight and patter.
]

,learning_outcomes: [
#text(size:12pt)[*Knowledge*]
- Recall the ab-initio technical content in full: crosswind effect on circuit tracking, crosswind takeoff technique, wing-down vs crabbed approach, aircraft/solo crosswind limits (per source lesson plan)
#text(size:12pt)[*Skills*]
- Teach wind correction angle application across every circuit leg, not just the final approach
- Demonstrate both wing-down and crabbed crosswind landing techniques with patter that explains when each might be preferred
- Teach the aircraft and solo crosswind limits explicitly, distinguishing them clearly for the simulated student
#text(size:12pt)[*Non-Technical Skills*]
- [HF] Patter the added cognitive load of crosswind correction on top of normal circuit workload
- [TEM] Model briefing crosswind component and technique selection before every approach, as the ab-initio lesson requires
- [ADM] Tighten the circle of confidence introduced in the Circuits walkthrough lesson — hands and feet closer to the controls than in calm-wind circuits, given the narrower margin for a late takeover
]

,theory_knowledge: [
- Ab initio source content in full: `phase2-stage-C2B - Crosswind Circuits` — Theory Knowledge and Ground Brief Topics sections
- FAA-H-8083-9A — Ch.3 (Effective Communication — teaching two valid techniques for the same problem, without confusing the student on which to use when, is a communication-clarity challenge worth reviewing before this lesson)
]

,ground_brief_topics: [
Total time: 40 min IC-prepared brief + IT questioning \
Equipment required: Whiteboard, runway diagram, aircraft model, the ab-initio source lesson plan (for IC preparation only — not for an IT demonstration) \

*IC-Prepared Brief* (40 min)
- IC prepares a full ground brief from `phase2-stage-C2B - Crosswind Circuits` alone, read and interpreted ahead of time: NTS, crosswind effect on circuit tracking, crosswind takeoff, wing-down and crabbed approach techniques, post-touchdown crosswind control
- IC delivers the brief; IT role-plays the assigned student capability
- IT questions the IC afterward on any content gaps or inaccuracies against the source lesson plan, and notes whether the IC clearly separated aileron's role (drift correction) from rudder's role (directional control) — the most commonly confused point in the ab-initio lesson's common-errors list
]

,ground_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor Trainer (IT)*]
- Confirm the IC has reviewed the C2B source lesson plan ahead of time (see Lesson 9 homework), without demonstrating the brief
- Choose and play a student capability level for the IC's brief
- Review weather, NOTAMs, and maintenance release with the IC
- Fly the full in-flight demonstration, covering both crosswind landing technique variants
#text(size:12pt)[*Instructor Candidate (IC)*]
- Present pertinent weather, NOTAMs, and maintenance release items to the IT
- Conduct the pre-flight inspection and fuelling as required
- Deliver the ground brief independently, without an IT demonstration first
- Observe the IT's in-flight demonstration
- Deliver the in-flight demonstration to the IT-as-student, covering both technique variants
]

,in_flight_tasks:[
#text(size:12pt)[*IT Demonstration*]
- Fly the full sequence from the ab-initio lesson plan with complete patter
#text(size:12pt)[*IC Recreation*]
- IC flies the identical sequence, delivering patter to the IT-as-student
- IC clearly briefs technique selection (wing-down or crabbed) before each approach, as the ab-initio lesson requires
]

,common_errors:[
- *Circle of confidence not tightened for crosswind*: The same principle from the Circuits walkthrough lesson applies with less margin here — coach the IC to stay closer to the controls than they would in calm-wind circuits, particularly through the flare and post-touchdown.
- *Aileron/rudder roles confused in teaching*: The ab-initio lesson itself flags this as the most common student error — the IC's patter must keep "aileron corrects drift, rudder keeps straight" unambiguous.
- *Only one technique taught*: Both wing-down and crabbed must be demonstrated — don't let the IC default to only their personal preference.
]

,post_flight:[
- *IC Self-Debrief*: IC critiques their own brief and demonstration.
- *IT Feedback*: Clarity of the aileron/rudder distinction in teaching; whether both technique variants were demonstrated; precision on the solo vs aircraft crosswind limit distinction; whether the circle of confidence was appropriately tightened for the conditions.
- *Next Steps*: Confirm readiness to progress to Forced Landing Without Power walkthrough. Log the student capability played this session.
]

,standards_for_progression:[
- IC's independently-prepared brief is accurate and complete against the source lesson plan, delivered without an IT demonstration to imitate
- IC tightens their circle of confidence appropriately for crosswind conditions, with no silent assistance observed
- IC keeps aileron (drift) and rudder (direction) roles unambiguous throughout teaching
- IC demonstrates and teaches both wing-down and crabbed technique
- IC states the solo vs aircraft crosswind limit distinction correctly
- *Outcome*: Recorded in IC training record (NC/C, Instructor Training Syllabus Elements 5.1, 6.2, 6.4, 9.2). "At or near" instructor standard is sufficient to progress.
]

,safety:[
- *Weather*: Lesson can go ahead if conditions are VMC (ceiling >1500 ft AGL, crosswind 5–15 kt for training, visibility >8 km). This lesson is conducted opportunistically when suitable crosswind is present — do not simulate crosswind conditions artificially.
- *Altitude*: Standard circuit height; strict monitoring of base-to-final given the steeper apparent turn in crosswind.
- *Emergency Procedures*: IT retains PIC authority throughout; EFATO brief before each takeoff.
- *TEM*: IT-as-student and IT-as-instructor distinction made clearly with "student/instructor taking over" and correct control handover — control is never shared or silently assisted; expect a tighter circle of confidence than in calm-wind circuits.
- *HF (4.1/4.4)*: IT confirms the IC managed session prep and time effectively (materials ready, brief timed, no last-minute scramble) and monitors both own and simulated-student fatigue/workload throughout, adjusting pace if either degrades.
- *Aircraft Limits*: 15 kt crosswind aircraft limit; do not exceed in training.
]

,homework:[
- Without reading the ab-initio FLWOP lesson plan, draft an original lesson plan for teaching the ABCDEF forced-landing-without-power sequence — referencing the RAAus Syllabus of Flight Training (V7) only, not the GCSFT ab-initio lesson plan — covering aim, learning outcomes, ground brief topics, common errors, standards for progression, safety, ready to brief from at the next walkthrough lesson.
- Prepare brief notes on the 6-S field selection criteria and be ready to recite ABCDEF fluently from memory.
]
)

#show: body => lesson_plan(metadata, lesson, body)
